<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eğirdir</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eğirdir is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isparta">Isparta</a> province, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, on the southern shore of Lake Eğirdir. In the 14th century it was the first capital of the Hamidid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>, and its Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserai</a>, converted into the Dündar Bey <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a>, survives from that period. The town and lake later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and are now known for fishing, apple orchards and tourism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/egirdir.jpg" alt="Eğirdir" width="320" height="138" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ingo Mehling · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Egirdir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Eğirdir lies near the south end of Lake Eğirdir, on a peninsula that extends northwards into the lake, about 110 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a>. A mountain called Eğirdirsivrisi, or Camili dağı, rises to 1,733 metres immediately to the southwest. To the northeast are two islands, Can Ada and Yeşil Ada, now joined to the mainland by a causeway. Yeşil Ada was formerly called Nis Ada, probably from the Greek word for island. The town was once called Eğridir, a Turkish form possibly borrowed from its old Greek name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akrotiri-prehistoric-city">Akrotiri</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Numerous ancient coins suggest an early settlement, and the ancient settlement of Prostanna was probably on the nearby mountain. The Greek name Akroterion is first attested in 1438 but was probably used earlier, in Byzantine times. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> first conquered the town around 1080, and it was probably taken with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isparta">Isparta</a> in 1204 by Kilij Arslan III of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a> probably built its citadel during his reign in the 1220s-30s.</p><p>In the 1300s Eğirdir, also known as Falakābād, became the capital of the Hamidoğulları beylik. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who visited in 1332, described a great and populous city with fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a>, running streams, mosques and orchards beside a lake of sweet water, and mentioned merchant shipping on the lake. The principality lasted until 1381. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> captured the town in 1403, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took control in 1417. Most of its population was Greek Orthodox until the population exchanges of the 1920s. Yeşil Ada once had a domed Christian basilica with frescoes dated stylistically to the 9th to 13th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Eğirdir is the seat of Eğirdir District in Isparta Province. Its castle is said to have been built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, with additions by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. Residents fish in the lake throughout the year, and Yeşil Ada keeps stone houses from the Greek era alongside restaurants and guesthouses. The town joined the Cittaslow network on 4 February 2017.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eğirdir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Felek al-Din Dundar Bey, grandson of Hamid Bey, a Turkmen commander of the Seljuks, established himself around <b>Egirdir</b> and Isparta at the beginning of the 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamidids">Hamidids</a></li></ul></section>
